---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import ToolPage from "@bt/ui/ToolPage.astro";
import AdSlot from "@bt/ui/AdSlot.astro";
import SueldoNetoCalc from "../../components/SueldoNetoCalc.tsx";
import { MX_2026, sueldoNeto } from "@bt/tools/mx";
import { money } from "../../components/form";

const fmt = money("en");
const pct = (r: number) => `${(r * 100).toLocaleString("en-US", { maximumFractionDigits: 3 })}%`;
const examples = [10000, 15000, 20000, 30000, 50000].map((g) => sueldoNeto({ monthlyGross: g }));
// Worked example: every figure comes from the calculator for $15,000.
const ex = sueldoNeto({ monthlyGross: 15000 });
const exRow = [...MX_2026.isrMonthly].reverse().find((b) => ex.monthlyGross >= b.lower)!;
const exExcess = Math.round((ex.monthlyGross - exRow.lower) * 100) / 100;
const exMarginal = Math.round(exExcess * exRow.rate * 100) / 100;
// Take-home pay by pay period. Weekly = monthly × 7 ÷ 30.4 (an approximation; payroll may round differently).
const periods = [10000, 12000, 15000, 20000, 25000, 30000].map((g) => {
  const n = sueldoNeto({ monthlyGross: g }).net;
  return { g, n, q: Math.round((n / 2) * 100) / 100, w: Math.round(((n * 7) / 30.4) * 100) / 100 };
});
---
<Layout
  bleed
  lang="en"
  title="Mexico net salary calculator 2026 (ISR and IMSS) | Baitaan"
  description="Work out your 2026 monthly take-home pay in Mexico: ISR from the SAT table, the employment subsidy and IMSS contributions. Free, no sign-up."
>
  <ToolPage
    heading="Mexico net salary calculator 2026"
    lead="Your net pay is your gross salary minus ISR (income tax) and IMSS (social security). If you earn up to $11,492.66 a month, the employment subsidy cuts your ISR by up to $535.65."
    updated="Updated: November 2026 · 2026 ISR table, UMA $117.31"
    sourcesHeading="Sources"
    sources={[
      { href: "https://www.sat.gob.mx/", label: "SAT: Annex 8 of the 2026 Miscellaneous Tax Resolution" },
      { href: "https://www.inegi.org.mx/temas/uma/", label: "INEGI: 2026 UMA value" },
      { href: "https://www.imss.gob.mx/", label: "IMSS: employer and employee contributions" },
    ]}
  >
    <SueldoNetoCalc client:load lang="en" />

    <Fragment slot="guide">
      <h2>How net pay is worked out</h2>
      <ol class="ms-5 list-decimal space-y-1">
        <li><strong>ISR:</strong> find your gross monthly salary in the SAT table. Apply your row's percentage to the amount above the lower limit, then add the fixed fee.</li>
        <li><strong>Employment subsidy:</strong> if you earn up to $11,492.66, take $535.65 off your ISR. The subsidy is never paid in cash: if it is larger than your ISR, your ISR is simply zero.</li>
        <li><strong>IMSS:</strong> multiply your contribution base (daily salary × integration factor) by 30.4 days and apply the employee rates.</li>
        <li>Subtract ISR and IMSS from your gross salary.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Step by step: a MX$15,000 salary</h2>
      <ol class="ms-5 list-decimal space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Daily salary:</strong> MX$15,000 ÷ 30.4 days = {fmt.format(ex.dailySalary)}.</li>
        <li><strong>ISR:</strong> MX$15,000 falls in the row that starts at {fmt.format(exRow.lower)}. The excess is {fmt.format(exExcess)}; at {pct(exRow.rate)} that is {fmt.format(exMarginal)}. Plus the fixed amount of {fmt.format(exRow.fixed)}: <strong>ISR of {fmt.format(ex.isr)}</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Employment subsidy:</strong> does not apply, because MX$15,000 is above the MX$11,492.66 ceiling.</li>
        <li><strong>IMSS:</strong> the contribution base is {fmt.format(ex.dailySalary)} × 1.0493 = {fmt.format(ex.sbcDaily)} a day. With the employee rates over 30.4 days: <strong>IMSS of {fmt.format(ex.imss)}</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Net pay:</strong> MX$15,000 − {fmt.format(ex.isr)} − {fmt.format(ex.imss)} = <strong>{fmt.format(ex.net)}</strong> a month.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>In other words, you keep about MX${Math.round((ex.net / ex.monthlyGross) * 100)} of every MX$100 you earn.</p>

      <h2>2026 examples</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="text-muted">
              <th class="py-2 text-start font-medium">Gross salary</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">ISR</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">IMSS</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Net pay</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {examples.map((r) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-hair">
                <td class="py-2 tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.monthlyGross)}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.isr)}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.imss)}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end font-semibold tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.net)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">With the legal minimum integration factor (1.0493). At $10,000 the subsidy is already applied.</p>

      <AdSlot unit="0000000000" label="Advertisement" />

      <h2>Net pay per month, fortnight and week</h2>
      <p>If you are paid fortnightly or weekly, this is what you keep each payday, before any other deductions:</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="text-muted">
              <th class="py-2 text-start font-medium">Gross monthly salary</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Net monthly</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Net fortnightly</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Net weekly (approx.)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {periods.map((r) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-hair">
                <td class="py-2 tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.g)}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end font-semibold tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.n)}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.q)}</td>
                <td class="py-2 text-end tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.w)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Other deductions you may see on your payslip</h2>
      <p>The calculator takes off only ISR and IMSS, which apply to almost every employee. Your payslip may also show:</p>
      <ul class="ms-5 list-disc space-y-1">
        <li><strong>Infonavit loan:</strong> if you have a housing loan, the repayment comes out of your pay.</li>
        <li><strong>Fonacot loan:</strong> consumer loans for workers are also collected through payroll.</li>
        <li><strong>Child support:</strong> when a judge orders it, the employer withholds it and pays it on.</li>
        <li><strong>Savings fund:</strong> it is your money; you get it back, usually at the end of the year.</li>
        <li><strong>Absences:</strong> days missed without permission are deducted from your pay.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>You can enter these amounts in the calculator's other-deductions field to see your real net pay.</p>

      <h2>2026 monthly ISR table</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="text-muted">
              <th class="py-2 text-start font-medium">Lower limit</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">Fixed fee</th>
              <th class="py-2 text-end font-medium">% on the excess</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {MX_2026.isrMonthly.map((r) => (
              <tr class="border-t border-hair">
                <td class="py-1.5 tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.lower)}</td>
                <td class="py-1.5 text-end tabular-nums">{fmt.format(r.fixed)}</td>
                <td class="py-1.5 text-end tabular-nums">{pct(r.rate)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>IMSS contributions paid by the employee</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Cash benefits: 0.25%</li>
        <li>Pensioners' medical costs: 0.375%</li>
        <li>Disability and life: 0.625%</li>
        <li>Old age and early retirement: 1.125%</li>
        <li>Sickness and maternity: 0.40%, only on the part of the daily salary above 3 UMA ($351.93)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In total, most people pay between 2.4% and 2.8% of their salary to IMSS.</p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Is ISR deducted if I earn the minimum wage?</summary><p>No. Anyone earning only the minimum wage ($315.04 a day in 2026) pays no ISR, and the employer covers their IMSS contributions.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>How do I work out my fortnightly net pay?</summary><p>Divide the monthly result by two. Your payslip may differ by a few pesos because employers calculate a fortnight as 15 or 15.2 days.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>What is the difference between gross and net salary?</summary><p>Gross salary is what your contract says. Net salary is what reaches your account after ISR, IMSS and any other deductions.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Why doesn't my payslip match exactly?</summary><p>Most often: your employer uses a different integration factor because it gives more benefits, counts a fortnight as 15 days instead of 15.2, or adds overtime, bonuses or absences. The difference is usually a few pesos; if it is large, ask HR for the breakdown.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Does the aguinaldo change my December net pay?</summary><p>The aguinaldo is free of ISR up to 30 UMA ($3,519.30 in 2026). Anything above that is taxed, so December's deduction can be bigger. Work out your aguinaldo with the <a class="text-link underline" href="/en/calculadora-aguinaldo/">aguinaldo calculator</a>.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>What is the integration factor?</summary><p>It turns your daily salary into your contribution base by adding the daily share of your aguinaldo and vacation premium. If your employer gives more benefits, your factor is higher.</p></details>
    </Fragment>
  </ToolPage>
</Layout>
